MultiResolutionMesh: make a saved proto mesh loadable again.

Neither half of the sub-mesh section table `MultiResolutionMesh::save_to_section`
writes could be read back by `load_from_section`, so saving a `.MRM` and
re-loading it took the process down with 0xC0000409 (measured against a
three-sub-mesh fixture; every retail `.MRM` in a Gothic II install loads fine,
so the reader is the side that matches the format).

1. `SubMesh::save` wrote each `MeshSection::size` as a *byte length*
   (`w->tell() - offset`) where `SubMesh::load` reads it as an element count —
   the number of triangles, wedges, colors, planes, edges and wedge-map entries
   to read. A saved sub-mesh therefore declared six times its triangles and
   twenty-four times its wedges, and the reader ran far past the end of the
   content region. The top-level position and normal counts were already
   element counts, so only the per-sub-mesh table was inconsistent.
2. The content-size word was measured from the word itself rather than from the
   first content byte. `load_from_section` reads the word and then seeks that
   many bytes forward from where the read left the cursor, so the sub-mesh
   section table was looked for four bytes past where it was written.

diff --git a/src/MultiResolutionMesh.cc b/src/MultiResolutionMesh.cc
index e3bed8c..8c00ff0 100644
--- a/src/MultiResolutionMesh.cc
+++ b/src/MultiResolutionMesh.cc
@@ -129,7 +129,11 @@ namespace zenkit {
 
 		auto off_end = static_cast<ssize_t>(w->tell());
 		w->seek(off_size, Whence::BEG);
-		w->write_uint(off_end - off_size);
+		// Measured from the first content byte, not from the size word itself:
+		// load_from_section reads this word and then seeks it forward from
+		// where the read left it, so counting the word made the sub-mesh
+		// section table start four bytes late.
+		w->write_uint(off_end - static_cast<ssize_t>(off_content));
 		w->seek(off_end, Whence::BEG);
 
 		w->write_ubyte(this->sub_meshes.size());    // submeshCount
@@ -264,6 +268,9 @@ namespace zenkit {
 		}
 	}
 
+	// `MeshSection::size` is an element count, not a byte length: SubMesh::load
+	// uses it directly as the number of triangles, wedges, edges and so on to
+	// read, and every retail `.MRM` parses correctly that way.
 	SubMeshSection SubMesh::save(Write* w) const {
 		SubMeshSection section {};
 
@@ -274,7 +281,7 @@ namespace zenkit {
 			w->write_ushort(tri.wedges[1]);
 			w->write_ushort(tri.wedges[2]);
 		}
-		section.triangles.size = w->tell() - section.triangles.offset;
+		section.triangles.size = static_cast<std::uint32_t>(this->triangles.size());
 
